A||||16||Fierochic88||88 GT Nasty Highway Driver?||||8||
Z||000000||Fierochic88||05-31-2001||06:11 PM||||Hey everybody-<BR>    I love my 88 GT to death but lately it's been giving me problems whenever I drive it <BR>at highway speeds.  Basically it's very jerky at these speeds.  The steering is very touchy.  The slightest move and you're swaying on the road.  I especially noticed this after I got done driving the 88 Formula my dad just rebuilt for a customer and the car outhandled mine by a lot.  My car seems to corner fine and is okay at lower speeds (under 45).  The car was just aligned and this seemed to accent the problem even more.  I know the alignment was correct so that is not the problem.  The tires are also in balance.  Any ideas what may be wrong?  I'm getting new rear tires soon but was wondering if it could possibly be anything else.  Thanks!<P>Jennifer<P>------------------<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<P>Thanks Phil - I forgot to add, the tire pressure in all 4 tires is okay.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Fierochic88 (edited 05-31-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||Phil||05-31-2001||06:12 PM||||Uneven rear tire pressure can exhibit those symptoms.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||05-31-2001||06:23 PM||||I'll try again- worn passenger side steering rack bushing($20 from Rodney Dickman)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Urchin||05-31-2001||09:54 PM||||Mine was handling very sloppy, as you are explaining.  I replaced the rear tie rod ends and it solved the problem.  Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_8.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||skitime||05-31-2001||09:58 PM||||I don't think 88s have rear tie rod ends.  My vote is for air pressures are wrong.  These cars handle wierd with wrong pressures.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pictures-1_5.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||PJ||05-31-2001||10:17 PM||||If the suspension is tight, your tires are true and your wheels balanced, your front caster is insufficient and/or unequal side to side. It takes more than $49.95 and the time most shops are willing to spend working on it to get it aligned right. The fact that it got worse after the alignment should tell you something.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||jerpro||05-31-2001||10:44 PM||||check for bent "a" arms and also check the ball joints and the bearings. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||06-01-2001||01:04 AM||||Pj, what would you consider to be "sufficient" front caster? Even at 5+ mine still does the "blowing in the wind" wandering.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||ray b||06-01-2001||01:32 AM||||there is a BAD alinement data for 88 in some shops computers<BR>front<BR>caster-5.0  +or-1/2<BR>camber0.0 +or- 1/2<BR>toe 1/16to 1/4 in <BR>rear is -1 +or-1/2camber<BR>1/16 to1/4 toe in<BR>did you get a print out <BR>ck tyres pressure its free <BR>try small changes 2-3 lbs not 5-10lbs<BR>jack and shake wheels ck for bad parts<BR>shocks working good? bounce 2-3 times then stop?<BR>roll bar end links still good?<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||06-01-2001||11:24 AM||||-5 caster? Isn't back + and forward -?<P>On my 86, the front caster is +2 with the stock washers (6/6mm). I found one of the 3/9mm kits and that brought it to 5. It certainly does improve the straight line stability, but it doesn't solve the wandering problem. <P>I made some 1/11mm washers that set the caster to about 9 degrees. That tightens it up even more, but you can now feel the steering effort increase in a turn. Dives into turns very nice, but it's a bit harder to steer into the turns. 5-7 is about the best. <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fierochic88||06-01-2001||12:45 PM||||Hey guyz, thanks for all the advise so far... I will pass it on to my dad as we inspect the car this weekend for possible problems.  I do know that the alignment is okay, as my friend that aligns the cars has several Fieros himself, and we have never had a problem before, I also have the before and after specs printed out...A definite difference.  Could it be possible that the alignment accentuated the problem?<P>------------------<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ray b||06-01-2001||01:05 PM||||i have hard copy of post by mwbackus on 3-27-00 that said front caster -4 1/2 to 5 1/2 with 5 optimum for 88's only.i guess that should be +4 not minus 4 sorry to repeat bad info.<BR>early cars are +3 to max of +7.0 front caster as posted by oreif on same date.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||06-01-2001||01:36 PM||||Was your car just rebuilt?<BR>If it wasn't, you cited the reason yourself in comparing your car to one that has been rebuilt.<P>How new are your bushings?<P>An alignment won't do any good if the car can't hold its alignment, which it can't on worn out bushings and/or ball joints.<P>One thing I've been wondering about for a while: How are the strut tops put together? Is there rubber in them that might be nice to replace with a spherical bearing or something?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Eric||06-01-2001||04:23 PM||||One time I had a front upper control arm come loose on me.  Of course the car didn't handle well because the alignment would change everytime I turned the wheel.  Bad lower ball joints will make the car want to track every road variation.  You may feel like you're falling off the highway as you cross the center stripe/ crown of the road.||||reg||1
Z||000014||PJ||06-01-2001||07:02 PM||||Terry, +5° caster is where I like mine to be. Most alignment equipment (and the Factory Manual) come with two caster specs for '88's, +5° for manual steering and +3° for GT's and Formulas. The +3° was for cars equipped with the power assisted steering that never made it into production, so all the '88's need +5°. Have you checked the rear toe lately? If the rear wheels are toed out the car will wander at highway speeds but be fine driving around town. Worn tie rods ('84-'87) could cause this as Urchin pointed out. I once did some bad math and toed out the rear on my '88. Scared myself silly.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fierochic88||06-01-2001||07:53 PM||||Okay.  The man of authority (my dad :-)) said thanks...a lot of good suggestions.  Couple more things to add...Struts and Strut tops are new...I will recheck the alignment specs...however the GoodYear tire rep looked at my rear tires today and said they are wearing perfectly and that I possibly got a bad tire in the back (It wore out after 5,000 miles of being "babied").  So another plus as far as the alignment is concerned.  Will recheck the specs though.  Thanks again!<P>Jennifer<P>------------------<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||terryk||06-01-2001||09:29 PM||||Hi PJ,<P>There is absolutely nothing that is not new in the suspension except the control arm steel itself. Name it, I replaced it.<P>It's a strange problem. When I find it, I'm writing it down.<BR>||||reg||1
